<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<handout>
    <title>Engineering Modeling Activity: Building and Solving Second-Order Differential Equations</title>

    <introduction>

        <p>
            Engineers frequently develop differential equations directly from
            physical systems. In this activity, your task is not only to solve
            differential equations but also to construct the mathematical model
            from a physical description.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each scenario:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Identify the relevant physical quantities.</li>
            <li>Write the governing physical law.</li>
            <li>Develop the differential equation.</li>
            <li>Identify the coefficients.</li>
            <li>Classify the roots of the characteristic equation.</li>
            <li>Solve the differential equation.</li>
            <li>Interpret the physical meaning of the solution.</li>
        </ol>

    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Reference Formulas and Hints</title>

        <p>
            The following engineering relationships may be useful.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <title>Newton's Second Law</title>

            <me>
                \sum F = m a = m\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}
            </me>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Hooke's Law (Spring Force)</title>

            <me>
                F_s=-kx
            </me>

            <p>
                where <m>k</m> is the spring constant.
            </p>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Damping Force</title>

            <me>
                F_d=-c\frac{dx}{dt}
            </me>

            <p>
                where <m>c</m> is the damping coefficient.
            </p>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Series RLC Circuit</title>

            <me>
                Lq''+Rq'+\frac{1}{C}q=E(t)
            </me>

            <p>
                where <m>q(t)</m> represents charge.
            </p>
        </definition>

    </subsection>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Undamped Spring-Mass System</title>

        <p>
            A 2 kg mass is attached to a spring with spring constant
            18 N/m. The mass is released from an initial displacement of
            0.10 m with zero initial velocity.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Draw a free-body diagram and identify all forces.
            </li>

            <li>
                Using Newton's Second Law and Hooke's Law, derive the
                differential equation governing the motion.
            </li>

            <li>
                Put the equation into standard form
                <m>x''+bx'+cx=0</m>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Form the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine the roots and classify them.
            </li>

            <li>
                Solve the initial value problem.
            </li>

            <li>
                Describe the resulting motion.
            </li>
        </ol>
<image width="50%">
    <shortdescription>Undamped spring-mass system.</shortdescription>

    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[thick] (-1,4) -- (1,4);

\draw[thick,decorate,decoration={coil,aspect=0.4,segment length=4pt,amplitude=4pt}]
(0,4) -- (0,1.8);

\draw[fill=gray!20] (-0.6,1) rectangle (0.6,1.8);

\node at (0,1.4) {$m$};

\draw[->] (1.2,1.4) -- (1.2,0.4);
\node[right] at (1.2,0.9) {$x(t)$};

\node[left] at (-0.3,3) {$k$};

\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Vibration Isolation Mount</title>

        <p>
            A 5 kg instrument package is mounted on a vibration isolation
            system consisting of a spring with spring constant
            80 N/m and a damper with damping coefficient
            40 N·s/m.
        </p>

        <p>
            The displacement from equilibrium is denoted by
            <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Write expressions for the spring force and damping force.
            </li>

            <li>
                Apply Newton's Second Law.
            </li>

            <li>
                Derive the governing differential equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Identify the coefficients
                <m>m</m>,
                <m>c</m>, and
                <m>k</m>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Classify the roots as distinct real, repeated, or complex.
            </li>

            <li>
                Explain whether the system oscillates.
            </li>
        </ol>
<image width="50%">
    <shortdescription>Spring-mass-damper system.</shortdescription>

    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[thick] (-1,4) -- (2,4);

\draw[thick,decorate,
decoration={coil,aspect=0.4,segment length=4pt,amplitude=4pt}]
(0,4)--(0,2);

\draw[thick] (1,4)--(1,3.1);
\draw[thick] (0.9,3.1)--(0.9,2.6);
\draw[thick] (1.1,2.6)--(1.1,3.1);
\draw[thick] (0.9,3.1)--(1.1,3.1);
\draw[thick] (0.93,2.8)--(1.07,2.8);
\draw[thick] (1,2.8)--(1,2);

\draw[fill=gray!20]
(-0.4,1)--(1.2,1)--(1.2,2)--(-0.4,2)--cycle;

\node at (0.4,1.5) {$m$};
\node[left] at (-0.2,3) {$k$};
\node[right] at (1.2,3) {$c$};

\draw[->] (1.4,1.5)--(1.4,0.3);
\node[right] at (1.4,0.9) {$x(t)$};

\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Critically Damped Door Closer</title>

        <p>
            An engineer is designing a hydraulic door closer. The angular
            displacement of the door is modeled by
            </p>

            <me>
                m\theta''+c\theta'+k\theta=0.
            </me>

        <p>
            The system parameters are
        </p>

        <me>
            m=1,
            \qquad
            c=10,
            \qquad
            k=25.
        </me>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Write the differential equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Compute the roots.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine whether the system is underdamped,
                overdamped, or critically damped.
            </li>

            <li>
                Find the general solution.
            </li>

            <li>
                Explain why engineers often prefer this behavior in
                automatic door systems.
            </li>
        </ol>
<image width="50%">
    <shortdescription>Door angular displacement diagram.</shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[thick] (0,0)--(0,3);

\filldraw (0,0) circle (0.08);

\draw[ultra thick] (0,0)--(3,1.5);

\draw[->] (0.8,0.37) arc (20:60:1);
\node at (0.9,0.85) {$\theta(t)$};

\node at (1.8,.5) {Door};

\node at (-0.8,1.5) {Frame};

\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Designing for a Desired Response</title>

        <p>
            A 4 kg machine component is mounted on a spring with
            spring constant
        </p>

        <me>
            k=64 \text{ N/m}.
        </me>

        <p>
            The engineer wants the system to be critically damped.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Write the differential equation
                involving the unknown damping coefficient
                <m>c</m>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Form the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine the value of
                <m>c</m>
                required for critical damping.
            </li>

            <li>
                Compute the repeated root.
            </li>

            <li>
                Write the corresponding general solution.
            </li>
        </ol>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>RLC Circuit Modeling</title>

        <p>
            A series RLC circuit contains
        </p>

        <me>
            L=2 \text{ H},
            \qquad
            R=8\Omega,
            \qquad
            C=\frac{1}{20}\text{ F}.
        </me>

        <p>
            After disconnecting the voltage source, the charge satisfies
        </p>

        <me>
            Lq''+Rq'+\frac{1}{C}q=0.
        </me>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Derive the differential equation governing the charge.
            </li>

            <li>
                Write the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Solve for the roots.
            </li>

            <li>
                Find the general solution.
            </li>

            <li>
                Compare the resulting equation with a spring-mass-damper
                system. Which term represents damping?
            </li>
        </ol>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Challenge Activity: Reverse Engineering a System</title>

        <p>
            An engineer measures the displacement of a vibrating system and
            determines that the motion is
        </p>

        <me>
            x(t)=e^{-3t}
            \left(
            C_1\cos(4t)
            +
            C_2\sin(4t)
            \right).
        </me>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Determine the characteristic roots.
            </li>

            <li>
                Construct the characteristic equation.
            </li>

            <li>
                Determine a differential equation having this solution.
            </li>

            <li>
                Identify values of
                <m>m</m>,
                <m>c</m>, and
                <m>k</m>
                if the equation is put into the form
            </li>
        </ol>

        <me>
            mx''+cx'+kx=0.
        </me>

        <p>
            Can multiple choices of
            <m>m</m>,
            <m>c</m>, and
            <m>k</m>
            produce the same solution?
            Explain.
        </p>

    </exercise>

    <subsection>
        <title>Reflection Questions</title>

        <ol>
            <li>
                How does increasing the damping coefficient affect the
                characteristic roots?
            </li>

            <li>
                What physical behavior corresponds to:
                <ul>
                    <li>distinct real roots,</li>
                    <li>a repeated root,</li>
                    <li>complex roots?</li>
                </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
                Why do oscillatory systems lead naturally to complex roots?
            </li>

            <li>
                What similarities did you observe between mechanical and
                electrical systems?
            </li>
        </ol>

    </subsection>



</handout>
